@.str.11 = private unnamed_addr constant [1536 x i8] c"\0A\0A**Store URL format**: `local`, *root*\0A\0AThis store type accesses a Nix store in the local filesystem directly\0A(i.e. not via the Nix daemon). *root* is an absolute path that is\0Aprefixed to other directories such as the Nix store directory. The\0Astore pseudo-URL `local` denotes a store that uses `/` as its root\0Adirectory.\0A\0AA store that uses a *root* other than `/` is called a *chroot\0Astore*. With such stores, the store directory is \22logically\22 still\0A`/nix/store`, so programs stored in them can only be built and\0Aexecuted by `chroot`-ing into *root*. Chroot stores only support\0Abuilding and running on Linux when [`mount namespaces`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/mount_namespaces.7.html) and [`user namespaces`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/user_namespaces.7.html) are\0Aenabled.\0A\0AFor example, the following uses `/tmp/root` as the chroot environment\0Ato build or download `nixpkgs#hello` and then execute it:\0A\0A```console\0A# nix run --store /tmp/root nixpkgs#hello\0AHello, world!\0A```\0A\0AHere, the \22physical\22 store location is `/tmp/root/nix/store`, and\0ANix's store metadata is in `/tmp/root/nix/var/nix/db`.\0A\0AIt is also possible, but not recommended, to change the \22logical\22\0Alocation of the Nix store from its default of `/nix/store`. This makes\0Ait impossible to use default substituters such as\0A`https://cache.nixos.org/`, and thus you may have to build everything\0Alocally. Here is an example:\0A\0A```console\0A# nix build --store 'local?store=/tmp/my-nix/store&state=/tmp/my-nix/state&log=/tmp/my-nix/log' nixpkgs#hello\0A```\0A\0A\00", align 1
